Hi Toby,

Thanks for replying

and good idea!

the attached patch (against R-devel r90842) adds predict(..., cache = TRUE)

on the span = 0.5 error, well, span was passed to predict(), which ignores it, so the fit still used 0.75. It needs to go to loess().

also, with or without the patch, the existing workspace limit stops se = TRUE at about N = 46,300 for span = 0.5 (37,800 for 0.75), so N has to stay below that

ares <- atime::atime(
  N = unique(as.integer(10^seq(1, log10(45000), by = 0.1))),
  setup = {
    set.seed(20261105)
    x <- runif(N, -3, 3)
    y <- sin(x) + rnorm(N, sd = 0.35)
    f <- loess(y ~ x, span = 0.5)
    nd <- data.frame(x = seq(min(x), max(x), length.out = 80L))
  },
  seconds.limit = 1,
  cache.FALSE = predict(f, nd, se = TRUE, cache = FALSE),
  cache.TRUE = predict(f, nd, se = TRUE, cache = TRUE))

in my run cache = FALSE hit the 1 s limit at N = 10,000, and both gave bit for bit identical predictions

the gap between the two memory curves is just the new table, which is now allocated with R_alloc as you suggested; the existing workspace still uses R_Calloc, which atime doesn't see

i guess the old search is probably what made predict(se = TRUE) grow with N^2. with cache = TRUE the time roughly doubles when N doubles (0.09 s to 0.17 s CPU from N = 20,000 to 40,000), while cache = FALSE roughly quadruples (1.1 s to 4.4 s from 10,000 to 20,000), which fits the log-linear plot you got. what still grows with N^2 is the workspace, and that is what limits N

Thanks,
Don


