Hi Toby,

Thanks for trying it

1. can you please modify the code so that quadratic workspace memory uses R memory allocation, so we can see quadratic memory usage in the atime figure?

yes, i tried moving the big workspace (iv and v in loess_workspace) to R_alloc. atime then shows the quadratic memory, but peak RSS went up about 3 times in my runs, so i'd use that as a measurement build only, i guess? i will think about this a bit further

2. Is there a way of reducing that workspace memory from quadratic to linear?

i think so. my understanding is that the workspace is quadratic because predict(se = TRUE) keeps the full vertex coefficient matrix, about (D + 1) x nf x nvmax doubles. we could keep a small record of each vertex's QR and rebuild the coefficients when they are needed? so the extra memory grows linearly with N; I added some iamges in:  https://github.com/tdhock/atime/issues/135

so basically if i understood it correct R now fits a small local regression at each vertex and then stores all the coefficients in one table sized for max(200, N) vertices, although with the default settings only a handful exist (17 in your setup). the new code does the same fits in the same order, whjoch is keeps the coefficients of the vertices that really exist up to 64 MB, and for any it can't keep it saves only the few numbers needed to redo that fit later. Same behaviour in gradient checkpointing (https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06174) and FlashAttention (https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135) which were what i was thinking about for this

so with your setup (one predictor, span = 0.5, 80 points) the new version is 2 to 6 times faster than the Nov 6 patch at the same N, its native workspace at N = 45,000 is 28 MB instead of 20 GB (peak RSS 0.15 GB vs 4.7 GB), and it keeps going past the old limit (N = 200,000 in 0.15 s CPU). predictions and L were bit for bit identical in every case i compared (will relaly appreciate some double checking if possible thanks!)

with 4 predictors my first version was up to 30% slower than the previous patch, but keeping the coefficients up to a fixed cap (64 MB) and rebuilding only the rest fixed that: over 60 settings with 1 to 4 predictors it's now faster in all of them (second figure). I am not sure whether the team would consider this tradeoff as worthy. when many x values are tied and the span is small, some neighbourhoods have zero radius (where R warns "zero-width neighborhood"), and in those cases it now gives exactly the same predictions, warnings and errors as current R.

i tested that it also stays bit for bit identical with three BLAS libraries (reference, Accelerate, OpenBLAS). the costs are that for some medium sized fits it can use up to about 28 MB more than the current workspace, and the patch is bigger (it adds renamed copies of dqrdc and dqrsl that also save a few scalars about 2k LoCs which is another trade off). I've only tested on a Mac, so if the approach sounds ok i'll tidy it up and send the patch

Thanks,
Don


